\section{Experimental Results}

This section provides quantitative evidence for the proposed journal analysis pipeline. All experiments were run with fixed data splits and reproducible seeds to ensure fair comparison across methods.

\subsection{Computational Environment}
Experiments were conducted on a Windows machine with the following configuration:
\begin{itemize}
	\item CPU: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-13420H
	\item GPU: Intel(R) UHD Graphics (CPU-only training/inference)
	\item RAM: 16 GB
	\item Python: 3.13.0
	\item Frameworks/Libraries: PyTorch 2.9.0+cpu, scikit-learn 1.6.1, pandas 2.2.3, NumPy 2.1.3
\end{itemize}

For the proposed neural components, the main training configuration was:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Train-test split: 80\% / 20\% (stratified)
	\item Epochs: 50 (main model training), 20 (tuning runs)
	\item Learning rate: 0.001 (default main training)
	\item Optimizer: Adam
	\item Batch size: 32 (tuning candidates: 16, 32, 64)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{A. Dataset Description}
The evaluation dataset is \texttt{combined\_dataset.csv}, a custom text dataset for cognitive distortion analysis.

\begin{itemize}
	\item Total usable samples: 4530 (after removing rows with missing text/label)
	\item Number of classes in raw labels: 15
	\item Binary target for distortion detection: distorted vs. no distortion
\end{itemize}

Raw label counts are:
\begin{itemize}
	\item No Distortion: 933
	\item Overgeneralization: 439
	\item Mind Reading: 439
	\item Magnification: 395
	\item Personalization: 353
	\item Fortune-telling: 343
	\item Emotional Reasoning: 334
	\item Labelling: 200
	\item Mental Filter: 200
	\item All-or-Nothing Thinking: 200
	\item Should Statements: 200
	\item Labeling: 165
	\item Mental filter: 122
	\item Should statements: 107
	\item All-or-nothing thinking: 100
\end{itemize}

For binary evaluation, labels were mapped as:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Distorted: 3597
	\item No Distortion: 933
\end{itemize}
which indicates class imbalance toward the distorted class.

The stratified split produced:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Training set: 3624
	\item Test set: 906
\end{itemize}

Because this is a text-based custom dataset, preprocessing included NA filtering, text casting to string, and TF-IDF feature extraction. Ethical considerations include anonymization and avoiding personally identifying information in journal samples.

\subsection{B. Evaluation Metrics}
For binary classification, the following metrics were used.

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Accuracy} = \frac{TP + TN}{TP + TN + FP + FN}
\label{eq:exp_accuracy}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Precision} = \frac{TP}{TP + FP}
\label{eq:exp_precision}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Recall} = \frac{TP}{TP + FN}
\label{eq:exp_recall}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{F1\text{-}score} = 2 \cdot \frac{\mathrm{Precision} \cdot \mathrm{Recall}}{\mathrm{Precision} + \mathrm{Recall}}
\label{eq:exp_f1}
\end{equation}

where $TP$, $TN$, $FP$, and $FN$ denote true positives, true negatives, false positives, and false negatives, respectively.

Conceptually:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Accuracy measures overall correctness.
	\item Precision measures how many predicted positives are truly positive.
	\item Recall measures how many true positives are recovered.
	\item F1-score balances precision and recall.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{C. Comparison with Baseline and State-of-the-Art Methods}
All methods were evaluated under the same data split, feature extraction protocol (TF-IDF with max\_features=500, ngram\_range=(1,3)), and test partition.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Performance comparison with baseline and proposed methods.}
\label{tab:method_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\hline
Method & Accuracy & Precision & Recall & F1-score \\
\hline
Logistic Regression & 0.9481 & 0.9680 & 0.9666 & 0.9673 \\
Linear SVM & 0.9393 & 0.9703 & 0.9527 & 0.9614 \\
Random Forest & 0.9525 & 0.9708 & 0.9694 & 0.9701 \\
Proposed Saved Model & 0.7936 & 0.7936 & 1.0000 & 0.8849 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Analysis:}
\begin{itemize}
	\item The proposed saved model achieved perfect recall (1.0000), meaning it detected all distorted samples in the test set.
	\item However, precision and accuracy are lower than the baselines, indicating many false positives.
	\item This behavior is consistent with threshold-sensitive models trained on distortion-heavy data: the model favors sensitivity over specificity.
	\item The strong baseline results (especially Random Forest and Logistic Regression) suggest that for this TF-IDF setup and dataset split, simpler discriminative classifiers generalize better than the deployed saved neural checkpoint.
\end{itemize}

Regarding fitting behavior, the original notebook reported very high synthetic-data distortion accuracy and moderate emotion accuracy. Combined with the lower real-data saved-model precision here, this indicates potential overfitting to synthetic patterns and dataset shift between training and evaluation distributions.

\subsection{D. Hyperparameter Tuning}
Hyperparameters were tuned through systematic iterative experiments using a validation split from the training set (stratified, seed=42). The tuning procedure evaluated multiple candidate values while keeping all other settings fixed.

\subsubsection{Learning Rate Tuning}
The learning rate was tested over $\{10^{-2}, 10^{-3}, 10^{-4}, 10^{-5}\}$ for a compact distortion MLP (Adam optimizer, 20 epochs, batch size 32).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Effect of learning rate on validation accuracy.}
\label{tab:lr_tuning}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\hline
Learning Rate & Validation Accuracy \\
\hline
0.01 & 0.9434 \\
0.001 & 0.9421 \\
0.0001 & 0.9531 \\
0.00001 & 0.7959 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

From Table~\ref{tab:lr_tuning}, the best validation accuracy was achieved at $10^{-4}$. A very small learning rate ($10^{-5}$) significantly reduced performance due to slow optimization progress.

\subsubsection{Batch Size Tuning}
Batch size was tested over $\{16, 32, 64\}$ with learning rate fixed at $10^{-4}$ and 20 training epochs.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Effect of batch size on validation performance.}
\label{tab:batch_tuning}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\hline
Batch Size & Validation Accuracy \\
\hline
16 & 0.9545 \\
32 & 0.9517 \\
64 & 0.9531 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The best validation score was obtained with batch size 16, while 32 and 64 produced comparable but slightly lower values.

\subsubsection{Final Configuration Justification}
Based on the tuning results:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Selected learning rate: $10^{-4}$ (highest validation accuracy)
	\item Preferred batch size: 16 (best validation performance)
\end{itemize}

This choice balances convergence stability and generalization. Larger learning rates showed less stable optima, while excessively small learning rates underfit within practical epoch budgets.

